    <h1>How to run payroll for your first employee</h1>
    <p class="prose-sub">Hiring your first person is a big step. Here's the checklist that gets them paid correctly, and on time.</p>

    <h2>Before the first day</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Get an Employer Identification Number (EIN)</strong> from the IRS if you don't have one. It's free and you can apply online.</li>
        <li><strong>Register with your state</strong> for state income tax withholding (if your state has an income tax) and for state unemployment insurance (SUTA). Your state will give you account numbers and your SUTA rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Decide how you'll pay</strong>: how often (weekly, every two weeks, twice a month or monthly), and whether by direct deposit or check. Some states set a minimum pay frequency.</li>
        <li><strong>Check workers' compensation</strong> insurance rules for your state; most require it once you have employees.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>On the first day</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Form W-4</strong>: the employee fills it in so you know how much federal income tax to withhold. Many states have their own withholding form too.</li>
        <li><strong>Form I-9</strong>: confirms the person can work in the US. The employee completes their part by the first day, and you review their documents within three business days of their start date.</li>
        <li><strong>New-hire report</strong>: report the new employee to your state's new-hire registry, generally within 20 days of hire (some states require it sooner).</li>
        <li><strong>Direct deposit details</strong>, if they want to be paid that way.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Running the first payroll</h2>
    <ol>
        <li>Add the employee with their pay rate, pay type (hourly or salary), W-4 and bank details.</li>
        <li>For hourly staff, make sure their hours for the pay period are entered and approved.</li>
        <li>Calculate payroll and review the paycheck: gross pay, each tax, deductions and net pay.</li>
        <li>Approve it, send the payment (direct deposit file to your bank, or print checks), then record it as paid.</li>
        <li>Set aside the taxes you withheld <em>and</em> your employer share (Social Security, Medicare and unemployment). These are deposited separately on the IRS and state schedules.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>After payday</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Deposit payroll taxes</strong> on time: federal deposits are usually monthly or semi-weekly depending on your size.</li>
        <li><strong>File quarterly returns</strong>: Form 941 for federal withholding and FICA, plus your state's returns.</li>
        <li><strong>At year end</strong>: give each employee a W-2 by January 31, and file Form 940 for federal unemployment.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep records</strong> of hours, pay and tax filings for at least four years.</li>
    </ul>

